{{c|{{smaller|''a'', Wool-sower gall, made by ''Andricus seminator''; ''b'', an individual cell (the gall is composed of many such cells).}}}}
usually an inch or more in diameter; the woolly material with which the cells are surrounded is rose-colored early in the season, but becomes rusty-brown toward the middle of the summer.

readings.c3s3:
¶
'''wool-sponge''' (wu̇l′spunj), ''n.'' A kind of bath-sponge, more fully called ''lamb’s-wool sponge.''

'''wool-staple''' (wu̇l′stā″pl), ''n.'' '''1.''' A city or town where wool was formerly brought to the king’s staple for sale.—'''2.''' The fiber or pile of wool. See ''staple''<sup>2</sup>, 7.

'''wool-stapler''' (wu̇l′stā″plėr), ''n.'' '''1.''' A dealer in wool; a wool-factor.
{{smaller block|They bought the foreign wool directly from the importer, and the native in the fleece, or from the ''wool-stapler.''
{{right|''English Gilds'' (E. E. T. S.), Int., p. clxxii.}}}}
'''2.''' A sorter of wool.

'''woolstock''' (wu̇l′stok), ''n.'' [< ''wool'' + ''stock''<sup>1</sup>, ''n.''] A heavy wooden hammer with a broad smooth face, employed in dressing woolen cloth.

'''woolward†''' (wu̇l′wärd), ''a.'' and ''adv.'' [Early mod. E. ''wolwarde''; < ME. ''wolward, wolleward, wulward''; lit. ‘against wool,’ i. e. with the skin against wool; < ''wool'' + ''-ward.''] With wool as clothing, especially next the skin: apparently always with the idea of doing penance by wearing an irritating and uncomfortable garment. {{smaller|—'''To go woolward,''' to wear uncomfortable clothing; specifically, to do penance, especially by wearing woolens next the skin.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>And wortes flechles wroughte & water to drinken,
And werchen & ''wolward gon'' as we wrecches vsen.</poem>
{{right|''Piers Plowman’s Crede'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 788.}}
<poem>Barefote and ''wolwarde'' I have hyght
Thyder for to ''go.''</poem>
{{right|''Lytell Geste of Robyn Hode'' (Child’s Ballads, V. 121).}}
I have no shirt; I ''go woolward'' for penance.
{{right|''Shak.'', L. L. L., v. 2. 717.}}
Poor people fare coarsely, work hard, ''go wolward'' and bare.
{{right|''Burton'', Anat. of Mel., p. 526.}}}}
